<title>RUSSIA: Half CIS states still in recession - statistics.</title>
<headline>Half CIS states still in recession - statistics.</headline>
<dateline>MOSCOW 1997-04-18</dateline>
<text>
<p>Half the 12 members of the Commonwealth of Independent States were still in recession in February, while growth has resumed in six former Soviet republics, the CIS Statistics Committee said on Friday.</p>
<p>Belarus and Georgia had the strongest growth in industrial output, while Moldova and Tajikistan suffered the biggest falls, the Interstate Statistics Committee said in its latest monthly bulletin.</p>
<p>Overall gross domestic product in the CIS in the first two months of this year was unchanged from a year earlier, after a four percent year-on-year fall in January-February 1996.</p>
<p>The committee issued the following data (pct change on year earlier, except inflation, which pct change on previous month)</p>
<p>			 GDP     industrial output    CPI inflation</p>
<p>		     Jan-Feb	  Jan-Feb		   Feb</p>
<p> Armenia	    +1.4	     -2.4		    +1.6</p>
<p> Azerbaijan	 +3.0	     -4.6		    +0.6</p>
<p> Belarus	   +10.0	    +12.3		    +6.6</p>
<p> Georgia	     n/a	     +6.6		    +2.1</p>
<p> Kazakhstan	  n/a	     +0.3		    +1.7</p>
<p> Kyrgyzstan	 +0.6	     +5.4		    +2.2</p>
<p> Moldova	     n/a	    -15.8		    +1.4</p>
<p> Russia	     +0.5	     +0.9		    +1.5</p>
<p> Tajikistan	 +4.7	    -14.5		    +1.4</p>
<p> Turkmenistan     -2.4	     -9.9		     n/a</p>
<p> Ukraine	    -9.9	     -6.8		    +1.2</p>
<p> Uzbekistan	  n/a	     +4.1		     n/a</p>
<p>--Jonathan Lynn, Moscow Newsroom, +7095 941 8520</p>
